  {
   "n": 3,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>Which of the following problems is decidable? (Here, CFG means context free grammar and CFL means context free language)</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<p>Given a CFG G, find whether <em>L</em>(<em>G</em>) = <em>R</em>, where <em>R</em> is regular set.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "<p>Given a CFG G, find whether <em>L</em>(<em>G</em>) = { }.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "Find whether the intersection of two CFLs is empty."
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "Find whether the complement of CFL is a CFL."
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "B, A, C, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(b)<br />\n(a) We don&rsquo;t have any standard algorithm to change CFG into CFL from a given CFG deciding a language is finite is decidable but regular its undecidable.<br />\n(b) From a given CFG we can determine the CFL and CFL emptiness is decidable.<br />\n(c) Intersection of two CFL is undecidable because it is not closed under intersection.<br />\n(d) CFL is not closed under Complement so its undecidable.</p>"
  },
  {
   "n": 4,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>Let M be the 5 stage NFA with ∈-transitions shown in the diagram below:</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/8419eb83c87dc14f15f4.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>Which one of the following regular expressions represents the language accepted by M?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "(00)* + 1(11)*"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "0* + (1 + 0(00)*) (11)*"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "(00)* + (1 + (00)*) (11)*"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "0+ + 1(11)* + 0(11)*"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "B, A, C, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Counter-example for option (a) : 011 is not generated.<br />\n&bull; Counter-example for option (c) : 000 is not generated.<br />\n&bull; Counter-example for option (d) : empty string is not generated.</p>"
  },
  {
   "n": 5,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33",
   "html": "<p>Which one of the following regular expressions is equivalent to the language accepted by the DFA given below?</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53516da84fd71fd4e41f.png\" /></p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "0* 1(0 + 10* 1)*"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "0* (10* 11)* 0*"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "0* 1 (010* 1)* 0*"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "0(1 + 0* 10* 1)* 0*"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "A, B, C, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0.33
   },
   "solution": "<p>(a)<br />\nThe given DFA accepts all and only binary strings containing odd number of 1&rsquo;s.<br />\nSo, option (a) is correct regular expression.<br />\nOption (b) Counter-Example: Generates empty string.<br />\nOption (c) Counter-Example: Doesn&rsquo;t generate 10011.<br />\nOption (d) Counter-Example: Doesn&rsquo;t generate 1.</p>"
  },
